<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Persistence - the Persistence service application project.

    Persistence is the ONLY service in the system that generates or executes SQL and the ONLY one
    that holds a storage provider. It hosts four gRPC services, contracts C-05 Query, C-06 Update,
    C-07 Command and C-08 Transaction, alongside two minimal REST endpoints on port 5101:
    /health anonymous, and /v1/ping which requires a JWT and answers 401 without one.

    Legacy provenance. These paths are the read-only behavioural oracle and are never referenced
    from the .NET tree, per constraint C-C; nothing here depends on the PowerBuilder toolchain,
    the PowerBuilder runtime, or any shipped native binary:
        ws_objects/pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl.src/                         the SQLite binding
        ws_objects/pfw.thread.pbl.src/                                 the async substrate
        ws_objects/pfw.thread.ext.pbl.src/                             the SQL task and transaction layer
        ws_objects/pfw.utility.parser.pbl.src/n_sql.sru, parsesql.srf   the SQL statement model

    WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT STATE.
    TargetFramework, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors,
    EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel and ManagePackageVersionsCentrally are all inherited from the
    repository-root Directory.Build.props by directory walk-up. Restating or overriding any of them
    here would be a defect. Package versions are inherited from the repository-root
    Directory.Packages.props, which is why every PackageReference below is VERSIONLESS: no version
    attribute, no floating range, no wildcard, no "latest".

    No project-level NoWarn or WarningLevel appears here either: warnings-as-errors is a quality
    gate, not a preference. Where a single analyzer diagnostic genuinely has to be relaxed for a
    preserved legacy identifier, the relaxation belongs in the repository-root .editorconfig scoped
    to the file that carries it. Inside this project that file is Sql/ClauseModifier.cs, and it is
    the only one.

    OutputType is left at the Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web default. OutputType=Exe is an xunit.v3
    requirement that applies ONLY to the sibling PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests project.

    No Protobuf item, no Grpc.Tools reference and no .proto include appears here.
    shared/PowerFramework.Contracts owns protocol-definition compilation, with a single Protobuf
    item at GrpcServices="Both". Compiling the same messages a second time would emit duplicate
    generated types and break the build; the stubs are consumed purely through the ProjectReference.

    Rules position. No user rules were provided for this project: review_rules returns exactly one
    line saying so. The binding constraints are therefore the enterprise-standard baseline plus the
    named non-rule constraints C-A through C-L, and each non-obvious decision below cites the
    constraint that drives it, as C-K requires.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Stated explicitly even though the containing directory name already yields it, because drift
      here is expensive: the parent Dockerfile invokes PowerFramework.Persistence.dll by name, and
      every .cs file across the ten subfolders declares a PowerFramework.Persistence.* namespace.
      AssemblyName is left to the SDK, which derives PowerFramework.Persistence from this project
      file's own name. A hyphenated form such as persistence-service is not a legal C# namespace and
      must never appear here.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Persistence</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Package references - versionless by design, see Directory.Packages.props">

    <!--
      The gRPC server for C-05 through C-08. Carries Grpc.Tools and Google.Protobuf transitively,
      so neither is referenced explicitly. gRPC is the right transport here because the legacy
      surface Query, Update, Exec, Commit, Rollback, SQLErrText is action-oriented rather than
      resource-oriented, server streaming reproduces progressive recordset delivery, and the
      mandated conflict response needs richer status detail than a plain status code carries.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Grpc.AspNetCore" />

    <!--
      Inbound token VALIDATION only, on /v1/ping and on every gRPC method, which is what C-G asks
      of a newly created boundary. Security is the sole issuer in the system, so the minting
      package Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens is deliberately absent: Persistence holds
      verification material and nothing more, and the JWKS fetch is performed by this handler
      itself, which is also why no outbound HTTP resilience package is referenced.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" />

    <!--
      SQLite is the only storage engine anywhere in the repository with a schema, DDL or connection
      string behind it, so it is the only provider provisioned. That is C-E: no database is
      fabricated. The SQL Server and Oracle statement generation the legacy carries survives as
      pure string transforms under Sql/Paging/, fully unit-testable with no instance of either
      engine running, so no client package for either is referenced and no SQL parser package is
      either. Encrypted SQLite is out of Phase-1 scope, so no SQLCipher package appears.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" />

    <!--
      C-K. ADO-level access is required IN ADDITION to the EF Core provider, and the reason is
      behavioural rather than stylistic. The legacy binding n_sqlite declares both Open(uri) and
      Open(uri, password), that is the test.db?mode=rwc[,password] URI grammar together with its
      documented check and journal extensions, and it executes statements through
      Exec(cmd, arg1 .. arg11) with positional binding. Neither the URI grammar nor the raw
      positional-? command execution that contract C-07 publishes is expressible through EF Core
      alone, so both layers coexist here on purpose.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" />

    <!--
      C-K, and mandatory rather than preferred. Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite pulls a 2.1.x
      native SQLite bundle transitively, and that version raises the high-severity NuGet audit
      advisory NU1903. Because TreatWarningsAsErrors is inherited as true, the advisory does not
      merely warn, it BREAKS the build. This explicit direct reference, centrally pinned at 3.0.5,
      overrides the transitive one and clears the audit: the resolved graph then reports the bundle,
      config, core and provider packages all at 3.0.5.

      It must NOT be marked PrivateAssets="all". The parent Dockerfile's runtime layer depends on
      this bundle's native e_sqlite3 being published beside the managed assemblies, and marking the
      reference private would strip it. Note also that the managed native comes from here and from
      nowhere else: no Content or None item copies the legacy sqlite3.dll or sqlite3.cipher.dll,
      which C-C forbids.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" />

    <!--
      Design-time only, for generating and maintaining the COMPANY schema migrations under
      Data/Migrations/. Marked as a development dependency in exactly the shape the SDK itself
      writes for this package, which declares developmentDependency in its own manifest:
      PrivateAssets="all" stops it flowing to consumers and keeps it out of the deployed payload,
      while IncludeAssets omits compile so no code in this project can bind to its types.

      C-K, recorded because the choice is genuinely not obvious. The runtime asset is retained
      deliberately. Dropping it removes Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design.dll from the build
      output, whereupon dotnet ef fails with "Your startup project does not reference
      Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" and the migrations this reference exists to produce
      become ungenerable. Retaining it costs nothing at deployment time, because PrivateAssets="all"
      already excludes the assembly from dotnet publish output and leaves no entry for it in the
      published deps.json, so the container image carries no design-time payload and no dangling
      dependency record. Both halves of that claim were verified by executing publish and
      inspecting the result rather than assumed.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Project references - shared libraries only, which is the whole of C-A">

    <!--
      The published boundary. Supplies the generated server base classes for C-05 QueryService,
      C-06 UpdateService, C-07 CommandService and C-08 TransactionService in namespace
      PowerFramework.Contracts.Persistence.V1, plus PowerFramework.Contracts.Common.V1 carrying
      RetCode, DwBuffer, ItemStatus, DbError and ConflictDetail.

      REVIEW INVARIANT, and the reason this reference and the next one deserve reading together.
      PowerFramework.Contracts declares no ProjectReference of its own, deliberately, so nothing
      anywhere in the repository enforces that the RetCode enum in common.v1.proto and the RetCode
      constants in Shared.Kernel agree numerically. This project is the first and only place both
      are referenced together, which makes it the only place such a divergence could ever surface.
      A mismatch is a silent wire-compatibility defect that no compiler will catch, so the two must
      be reconciled by inspection whenever either side changes.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\shared\PowerFramework.Contracts\PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj" />

    <!--
      RetCode including the complete SQLITE_* set, Enums supplying SQL_MS_REPLACE, SQL_MS_APPEND
      and SQL_MS_PREPEND together with the database-type constants the paging rewriters dispatch
      on, Predicates with the tri-state boundary semantics preserved verbatim so a prevention still
      reads as a success and cancelled is still neither succeeded nor failed, Formatting, and Bits.
      Bits is genuinely used, not incidental: the legacy packs retrieval progress current and total
      into a single long as two 16-bit words, and MakeLong reproduces that packing.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\shared\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />

    <!--
      Assertions and AssertionFailure. The static class is named Assertions rather than Assert,
      because a static class cannot contain a member of its own name; the file is Assert.cs and the
      Assert and AssertFailed method names are preserved. Persistence catches AssertionFailure so
      that the legacy fail-fast posture survives as fail-fast: a structural fault terminates,
      it never degrades gracefully.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\shared\PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics\PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics.csproj" />

    <!--
      OrderedMap, the substitute for the legacy n_map, used as the datastore cache by
      Tasks/SqlTaskBase.cs and Tasks/SqlQueryTask.cs. Its positional accessors are one-based with
      an inclusive Count bound, matching the legacy indexing convention, which is exactly the
      translation hazard the whole port has to respect rather than silently normalise.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\shared\PowerFramework.Shared.Containers\PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.csproj" />

    <!--
      EventBroker, VetoResult and EventArgumentContext. Required rather than convenient: AAP 0.4.1
      assigns the whole of pfw.thread to Persistence in scope, and one of that library's six objects
      is n_cst_threading_eventful.sru, which derives from n_cst_eventful. That inheritance is one of
      the two structural facts the AAP cites as proof that n_cst_eventful must live in a shared
      in-scope library at all, so the derived threading broker cannot be ported here without the
      base it derives from. Tasks/TaskProxies/ThreadingEventBroker.cs is that port, and it is the
      dispatcher behind the notification channels in Tasks/TaskProxies/SqlTaskProxyBase.cs.
      docs/SERVICE_MAPPING.md records object 233 as In scope / Persistence / Ported, and this edge is
      what makes that record true. Shared.Eventful declares exactly one project edge of its own, to
      Shared.Kernel, which is already referenced above - so this edge adds no new transitive project to
      the graph and the graph stays acyclic.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\shared\PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful\PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful.csproj" />

    <!--
      Deliberately absent. PowerFramework.Shared.Localization is not referenced because Persistence
      consumes no localized text: the diagnostics this service emits are operator-facing and the
      legacy strings it reproduces are hardcoded at their own sites, so an unused reference would be
      an unused coupling. No peer service project is referenced either: C-A permits the published
      contracts project as the only cross-service edge in the system, and no behavioural code
      crosses a service boundary. Nothing in the five paths above reaches outside shared/.

      A SECOND ProjectReference TO Shared.Eventful SAT HERE AND HAS BEEN REMOVED. It was an exact
      duplicate of the edge declared immediately above this comment, so MSBuild deduplicated it and the
      build never complained - which is precisely why it survived. The cost was not a build failure but a
      project graph that read as though this service took six edges where it takes five, directly under a
      comment whose subject is a reference that is deliberately ABSENT. One edge, one declaration, one
      place to change it.
    -->

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test visibility - what makes the per-service coverage gate reachable (C-H)">

    <!--
      Lets the sibling test project exercise the internal seams the coverage gate depends on: the
      injected clock behind the transaction pool's idle expiry, the internals of the SQL Server and
      Oracle paging rewriters, and the changeset and full-state codecs with their preserved defects.
      Without this the 80% line-coverage gate is reachable only by widening the public API, which
      would be the wrong trade. The SDK turns this item into the assembly attribute at build time.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests" />

  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
